EXPERIMENT 9 – EXCEPTION HANDLING USING JUNIT

AIM
To write JUnit test cases to test the exception handling of a division program using assertThrows().
ALGORITHM
1. Create a Maven project and add the JUnit 5 dependency.
2. Create a class named Calculator.
3. Write the divide(int a, int b) method.
4. Throw ArithmeticException when the divisor is zero.
5. Create CalculatorTest and verify valid division using assertEquals().
6. Use assertThrows() to test division by zero and verify the exception message.
7. Run the tests using mvn test.
CODE
Calculator.java
public class Calculator {
    public int divide(int a, int b) {
        if (b == 0) {
            throw new ArithmeticException("Cannot divide by zero");
        }
        return a / b;
    }
}


CalculatorTest.java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

public class CalculatorTest {
    private final Calculator calc = new Calculator();

    @Test
    void validDivision() {
        assertEquals(5, calc.divide(10, 2));
    }

    @Test
    void divisionByZeroThrowsException() {
        ArithmeticException ex = assertThrows(
            ArithmeticException.class,
            () -> calc.divide(10, 0)
        );
        assertEquals("Cannot divide by zero", ex.getMessage());
    }
}


